<main id="main" class="wrap web-page"><header><p class="eyebrow"><a href="web.html">Nexo Web</a></p><h1>Development tools</h1><p class="lede">Create a project, run it locally and inspect compiler diagnostics.</p><nav aria-label="Web guides"><p><a href="web-guide.html">Components and routing</a> · <a href="web-api.html">API and data</a> · <a href="web-development.html">Development</a> · <a href="web-deployment.html">Deployment</a></p></nav></header><section id="development"><h2>Develop with Vite</h2>
<p>Vite supplies the optional local development server and module transport. The Nexo compiler still owns parsing, types and browser code. No React wrapper or TypeScript application syntax is involved.</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>New project</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code>python -m nexo web new my-app
cd my-app
npm ci
python -m nexo web dev app.nexo
# Open the loopback URL printed by the command.
python -m nexo web check --json app.nexo
python -m nexo web audit --security --json app.nexo
python -m nexo web build app.nexo --sourcemap -o dist
python -m nexo web preview dist</code></pre></div>
<p>Install Nexo first. Development requires Node 22.12 or 24+ and locked Vite 8.3.5; npm installation is explicit. Existing repository examples use <code>npm ci --prefix nexo/web_tools</code> followed by <code>python -m nexo web dev examples/web/counter.nexo</code>. Build, check, audit and preview do not need Node.</p>
<p>CSS-only updates keep the UI/state. Compatible component logic edits refresh only affected boundaries and retain their own typed state; unaffected branches keep DOM identity. Descendants/providers/requests owned by a refreshed boundary are recreated. State/prop/provider shape, context, page, layout or routing changes safely remount the application. Compile errors retain the last valid UI and display a keyboard-readable text diagnostic. <a href="web-demo/">The working Counter</a> is the same source used by the scaffold and HMR tests.</p>
<p>Dev binds 127.0.0.1, checks Host/Origin, and does not serve project .env files, public directories or arbitrary source paths. It loads no project Vite configuration. Preview serves only built assets. Neither is production hosting or a backend API proxy; keep using the local API example for complete request/mutation testing.</p>
<h3>Find errors in the editor and browser</h3><p><code>web check --json</code> now includes source statement columns and snippets. <code>build --sourcemap</code> emits deterministic line-level JavaScript and CSS maps, including original local CSS imports and embedded sources; omit maps when source publication is not intended. Release CSS maps compose through Lightning CSS. Precise expression stepping remains unsupported.</p>
<p>Configure a frontend editor to run <code>python -m nexo web lsp</code>. It offers unsaved-entry diagnostics, local definitions/hover, outline and canonical browser completions. The existing core <code>nexo lsp</code> is unchanged. Browser formatting, cross-file navigation and unsaved imported-buffer overlays are not implemented.</p>
<h3>Review browser security boundaries</h3><p><code>web audit --security</code> applies compiler capability checks and flags credential-like literals and opt-in cache requiring identity review. Findings are redacted; no findings does not prove security. Backend authorization remains explicitly unassessed. The report explains Shield memory dispatch and bounded loopback ingress limits; it does not run Shield in a browser or turn its listener into production hosting.</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/marcrabadan/nexo/blob/main/docs/rfc/0001-nexo-web.md#m4">Development semantics, limits and troubleshooting</a>. Development features are tested locally with real Vite and Chromium; no comparative performance or production-readiness claim is made.</p></section><section><h2>Build and run locally</h2><div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>Local development</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code>python -m nexo web check --json examples/web/counter.nexo
python -m nexo web build examples/web/counter.nexo -o /tmp/nexo-counter
python -m http.server 8765 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory /tmp/nexo-counter
# Open http://127.0.0.1:8765/
python -m pytest tests/test_web_frontend.py</code></pre></div>
<p>Install Nexo from the repository first. Ordinary build emits <code>index.html</code>, <code>main.js</code>, <code>app.js</code> and <code>app.css</code>. Serve over HTTP; file URLs cannot reliably load ES modules. Development tools include <code>web new/dev/preview/audit/lsp</code> and opt-in JavaScript source maps. Release builds add <code>--release</code> optimization, integrity manifests and measured quality gates.</p>
<p>Diagnostics: NW1001 grammar, NW1002 names/types, NW1003 CSS boundaries, NW1004 unsafe DOM, NW1000 I/O. Composition diagnostics: NW2001 modules, NW2002 props/composition, NW2003 routes, NW2004 context and NW2005 slots. Data diagnostics: NW3001 JSON contracts, NW3002 request/readiness rules and NW3003 API URL boundaries. Tooling diagnostics: NW4001 tool availability, NW4002 dev boundaries, NW4101 credential review and NW4102 cache review. Release diagnostics: NW5001 release integrity/optimization and NW5101–NW5103 source accessibility findings. Each compile error gives a fix. Inspect JSON diagnostics before changing code.</p></section></main>
